# Copy to .env and edit. Configuration is read ONCE at boot: after any change run
# `docker compose up -d` to recreate the app container.
#
# compose.yaml interpolates EVERY variable of DESIGN.md §4 with the default shown
# here (plus a few compose-only knobs, marked as such), so this file is the
# complete list — anything commented out is already in effect at that value, and
# uncommenting it changes nothing until you edit it.
# The operator guide is DEPLOY.md (§7 configuration, §15 backup, §16 upgrades).

# --- the one you must get right ---------------------------------------------

# Absolute origin (scheme://host[:port], no path). Drives every absolute URL in
# share cards and API responses, the cookie Secure flag and og:video:secure_url.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8481

# --- storage ----------------------------------------------------------------

# WHERE THE MEDIA LIVES: a plain DIRECTORY on this machine — a disk, or a NAS
# share you have mounted on the host — which is the DEFAULT since DESIGN.md §20
# erratum 345, or a bucket of your own (the block "bring your own bucket" below)
# (DEPLOY.md §32, ROADMAP #37).
#
# The default stack is one container: no object-storage server, no second set
# of credentials, and no S3_* line at all — on STORAGE_BACKEND=fs any one of the
# four that name a bucket (S3_ENDPOINT, S3_BUCKET, S3_ACCESS_KEY, S3_SECRET_KEY)
# is a boot error naming itself, which is what lets an operator see from `.env`
# alone that this instance has no outbound destination for media at all.
#
# STORAGE_DIR is the path INSIDE the container; STORAGE_DIR_HOST is the path on
# the host (or the NAS mount point) that compose.yaml binds to it.
# STORAGE_DIR may not be, contain or sit inside DATA_DIR (/data) or SCRATCH_DIR
# (/scratch): those two are the small rebuildable ones, and the media store is
# the one you have to back up yourself.
#
# STORAGE_DIR_HOST has a DESKTOP DEFAULT: your own Videos folder, `~/Videos/clippet`
# (DEPLOY.md §32.1). compose.yaml spells it from HOME, or USERPROFILE on Windows,
# because compose interpolation cannot expand `~`; compose creates the directory on the
# first `up` (on Linux owned by root: give it to uid 10001, DEPLOY.md §2). A SERVER
# sets the line below. Set it HERE and not in your shell: compose reads this file
# itself, while an exported value on Git Bash is rewritten by MSYS into a path under
# `C:/Program Files/Git/…` before docker sees it. `admin doctor`'s `store` row prints
# the host directory it resolved to and which variable supplied it.
#
# The first two lines below are in effect at those values; the third is an example
# (unset, the Videos folder above is used). `COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.fs.yaml`
# still works and PINS this shape (DEPLOY.md §32.1). Unset, the app follows the
# four S3_* lines: none set is the folder, any set is a bucket — so a `.env`
# written before erratum 345 keeps its bucket with no new line.
#STORAGE_BACKEND=fs
#STORAGE_DIR=/media
#STORAGE_DIR_HOST=/srv/clippet-media
# (STORAGE_DIR_HOST_SET is computed by compose from the line above and is not yours
# to set: it is the one bit `admin doctor` needs to tell "you named this path" from
# "the desktop default supplied it".)

# THE DROP FOLDER (DEPLOY.md §34, ROADMAP #42): a video left at the ROOT of
# STORAGE_DIR — never under `clips/` — becomes a clip. Off unless this names the ONE
# account that should own whatever arrives; that account must already exist (a name
# that matches none is a boot error, like a bad DISCORD_APP_ID). It works on EITHER
# backend, because what it needs is a folder rather than a bucket: on
# STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 set STORAGE_DIR to a folder bound into the container and the
# clips still go to the bucket. Naming an owner with no STORAGE_DIR is a boot error
# naming STORAGE_DIR. A file is taken only once it has finished being written
# and ffprobe accepts it as a video; anything else is left exactly where it is. On
# failure the file stays put with a `<name>.failed.txt` beside it saying why.
#
# To bring in a folder of clips you ALREADY have, there is a one-time verb instead:
# `clippet admin import <dir> --owner <name>` (DEPLOY.md §34.4). It reads the folder
# and changes nothing in it, and running it twice imports nothing the second time.
#DROP_FOLDER_OWNER=alice

# TIMED BACKUPS (DEPLOY.md §15.6). Off unless BOTH of the first two lines are set;
# one without the other is a boot error. Every BACKUP_EVERY the server writes a
# snapshot of the database, never the media, into BACKUP_DIR and keeps the newest
# BACKUP_KEEP (30 when unset). BACKUP_DIR is a directory inside the container that
# already exists: the app never creates it. The file holds password and token
# hashes; keep it where you keep passwords.
#BACKUP_EVERY=24h
#BACKUP_DIR=/data/backups
#BACKUP_KEEP=30

# THE DATABASE SNAPSHOT IN THE STORE (DEPLOY.md §15). On by default: a copy of the
# database follows the clips, as <S3_KEY_PREFIX>_clippet/db.sqlite — in the bucket,
# or in the media folder above — at most once per DB_SNAPSHOT_EVERY after a clip
# changes, so the store alone can bring this instance back on another PC
# (`clippet admin restore --from-store`). On the folder that makes a backup of the
# folder the whole backup. The file holds password and token hashes: the bucket's
# keys, and every copy of the folder, ARE the instance.
#DB_SNAPSHOT=off
#DB_SNAPSHOT_EVERY=10m

# --- bring your own bucket (optional) ----------------------------------------

# A bucket of YOUR OWN instead of the folder: Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, Amazon
# S3, or any other S3-compatible server you run or rent. It unlocks the three
# things a folder cannot do: MEDIA_DELIVERY=redirect (DEPLOY.md §33), the
# database snapshot in the bucket, and `admin restore --from-store` from it
# (DEPLOY.md §15). The bucket must already exist; nothing here creates one. The
# /setup wizard prints the whole block for each provider (DEPLOY.md §35), and
# the provider matrix is DESIGN.md §4 (R2 wants S3_REGION=auto and
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false).
#
# Uncomment these lines TOGETHER. COMPOSE_FILE selects the bucket flavour,
# compose.nobucket.yaml: the app alone, no folder bind, and STORAGE_BACKEND=s3
# as its default (DEPLOY.md §35.2, DESIGN.md §20 errata 298 and 345). ASSIGN
# that line rather than deleting one: COMPOSE_FILE is read by compose on the
# HOST and never reaches the container, so nothing in the app can see it, warn
# about it or check it afterwards. The /setup wizard's blocks assign it for
# exactly this reason.
#
# An EMPTY value is a boot error naming it: `S3_ACCESS_KEY=` with nothing after
# the `=` on STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 does not fall back to anything (there is no
# bundled server to fall back to since erratum 345). `docker compose config |
# grep S3_` shows what the container will actually get (DEPLOY.md §7).
#
# S3_REGION's and S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE's comments are NOT their live values:
# compose's defaults are `us-east-1` and `true` (what every earlier stack
# resolved to); the values shown are R2's.
#COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR=:
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.nobucket.yaml
#STORAGE_BACKEND=s3
#S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#S3_REGION=auto
#S3_BUCKET=clips
#S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#S3_SECRET_KEY=
#S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false

# Optional prefix so one bucket can hold more than this instance (a trailing
# slash is added if you leave it off).
#S3_KEY_PREFIX=

# Per storage call (List/Stat/Remove/Ping and the response-header wait of every
# GET/PUT); how long a transfer may make no progress before it is aborted; and
# the absolute cap on one upload or download.
#STORAGE_OP_TIMEOUT=30s
#STORAGE_IDLE_TIMEOUT=2m
#STORAGE_TRANSFER_MAX=6h

# How many 16 MiB parts one large upload to the bucket sends at the same time.
# Memory: about this many x 16 MiB per running upload. 1 = one part at a time
# (the behaviour before DESIGN §20 erratum 337). S3 only; 1 to 32.
#STORAGE_UPLOAD_THREADS=6

# How hard the app tries to reach the store AT BOOT before it logs
# "storage unreachable; serving degraded" and carries on (the site is already
# up by then; only media needs the store). Raise it for a bucket or NAS that is
# slow to appear after a reboot; lower it to have a dead endpoint named in the
# log in seconds instead of a minute. Under 1 attempt is a boot error.
#STORAGE_PING_ATTEMPTS=10
#STORAGE_PING_INTERVAL=3s

# --- media delivery: bytes through the app, or a redirect (DEPLOY.md §33) ----

# proxy (the default, and every version before this one): the app streams every
# media byte itself. redirect: the same URLs, after the same gates, answer 302
# to a freshly presigned link on the bucket's own origin — so no video byte
# crosses whatever sits in front of the app. The share link, the card and the
# watch page are unchanged either way. `redirect` needs a bucket: with
# STORAGE_BACKEND=fs it is a boot error, because a directory has no URL.
#MEDIA_DELIVERY=redirect

# How long one of those signatures lasts (1m … 24h). A <video> keeps fetching
# the SAME src while its page is open, so a short TTL breaks seeking mid-watch.
#MEDIA_PRESIGN_TTL=1h

# The origin VIEWERS reach the bucket at, when that is not S3_ENDPOINT. SigV4
# signs the host, so a link minted for a private name (a server the app reaches
# by its compose service name or a LAN address) is valid nowhere else. On
# R2/B2/S3 leave it unset: one origin serves both.
#MEDIA_PRESIGN_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com

# --- clips: lifetime and limits ---------------------------------------------

# TTL applied when an upload does not choose one: never | 1d | 7d | 30d | 90d.
DEFAULT_CLIP_TTL=never

# Sweeper tick, the grace a clip mid-transcode gets past its expiry, and the
# most clips one tick will destroy (DEPLOY.md §10).
#SWEEP_INTERVAL=60s
#SWEEP_GRACE=1h
#SWEEP_BATCH=50

# Hard limit on the uploaded video (4 GiB) → 413. There are no per-user quotas:
# this is your disk. And the free space ingest insists on keeping spare.
#MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=4294967296
#SCRATCH_MIN_FREE_BYTES=536870912

# One chunk of the web upload, in bytes (DEPLOY.md §7): a file larger than one
# chunk goes up as several requests, because the edge in front of a public name
# refuses any one request body over its plan's cap (100 MB on Cloudflare Free
# and Pro). The edge's cap minus headroom; 5242880 … 95000000.
#UPLOAD_CHUNK_BYTES=52428800

# An ADVISORY line on how much this instance stores, in bytes; 0 (the default)
# is off. By default it is NEVER a cap: no upload is ever refused because of it,
# unless you set STORAGE_BUDGET_HARD=1 below. There are still no per-user quotas
# and no tiers either way — this is one line for the whole instance. When the
# bytes this database says are in the bucket pass it, the sweeper logs one WARN,
# /healthz grows a `storage_budget` object, the library page carries one line and
# /settings' Storage panel leads with the quota (DEPLOY.md §25). 200 GB would be
# 200000000000.
#STORAGE_BUDGET_BYTES=0

# …and the one opt-in that turns that line into a CAP (DEPLOY.md §25). With 1, a
# NEW clip is refused with `507 storage_quota` once the stored bytes reach
# STORAGE_BUDGET_BYTES — on the upload routes, the capture endpoint and the
# Discord bot alike, all with one sentence. A replacement or a trim of a clip
# that already exists is NOT refused: it adds no clip, and it is usually how you
# get back under the line. Refusing nothing is the default on purpose: an
# operator who set a budget to be WARNED must not discover it had quietly
# stopped their friends uploading. `1` without STORAGE_BUDGET_BYTES is a boot
# error, because a cap of zero would refuse everything.
#STORAGE_BUDGET_HARD=0

# What your provider charges you, per GB, in YOUR OWN currency (DEPLOY.md §31).
# Both are optional and both are unset by default, and unset means there is NO
# cost line anywhere — absent, never a 0.00, because a zero nobody configured
# reads as "storage here is free". `0` IS a number and prints as one: that is
# how a friend on R2, which bills storage and not egress, records it.
#
# Nothing is ever asked of a provider — no API, no price lookup, no second
# credential. The app multiplies these by bytes it already counts: what the
# database says is stored, and a per-UTC-day counter of what it has SENT (which
# holds nothing about any viewer — a date and a number of bytes, nothing else).
# Every figure is an ESTIMATE at 1 GB = 10^9 bytes, the decimal gigabyte
# providers bill in, and appears in `clippet admin stats` and in one Storage
# panel on /settings. NOT on /healthz: that route is anonymous, and a cost
# printed beside the bytes it came from would publish what you pay per GB to
# anyone who can reach the origin, so it carries the byte counts alone.
#
# Plain decimals, at most six digits after the point; no sign, no exponent, no
# separator and no currency symbol — anything else is a boot error naming the
# variable.
#STORAGE_PRICE_PER_GB_MONTH=0.015
#EGRESS_PRICE_PER_GB=0.01

# …or name a PRESET and skip the typing (DEPLOY.md §31.5): local | r2 | b2 | s3.
# A preset is a dated row of that provider's own list prices, typed into the
# image by a person who read their pricing page on the date the row carries —
# there is still no lookup of any kind, and the panel and `admin stats` say which
# list and when, so you can tell an estimate from a quote. It also carries the
# provider's FREE ALLOWANCES, which are the part nobody remembers: R2 and B2 each
# give you 10 GB of storage free for ever; R2 bills no egress at all, B2 gives
# you 3x your stored bytes free each month and S3 gives you 100 GB. All of those
# are subtracted before anything is priced. `local` prices everything at 0 — a
# disk you own has no provider bill, and that IS the number.
# List prices drift a few per cent a year: either of the
# two variables above OVERRIDES its half of the preset (both allowance rules
# stay), which is how you correct one without waiting for a new release.
# Unset (the default) = the custom case: your two typed prices and nothing else.
#STORAGE_PRICING=r2

# How long POST /api/upload (the capture-tool endpoint) waits before it answers,
# so the link ShareX puts on the clipboard already unfurls as a PLAYER card
# rather than a bare link — Discord caches the embed it extracts, so a link
# pasted while the clip is still queued can stay a link-out card long after the
# clip is ready (DEPLOY.md §9, §27).
#
# What it waits for is the CARD, not merely a playable clip: the poster and the
# file the card's og:video points at (decided 2026-09-08: a link is given only
# when the clip is ready for sharing, which means its Discord card is ready).
# That is why the default is 30s and not 8s.
#
# 0 turns the wait off. 60s is the hard ceiling, and AT the ceiling the link is
# still returned — with `card_ready: false` in the reply, and no estimate of how
# much longer it will be. There is no per-request override any more: a client
# that could send `wait=0` could configure the card away for everyone reading
# its links, and the field is now a 400 (§20 erratum 212).
#CAPTURE_READY_WAIT=30s

# --- transcode ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Simultaneous ffmpeg processes: the number of transcode loops, and the size of
# the pool every trim and GIF encode also takes a slot from. 1 .. 16; anything
# outside that is a boot error, because every slot is a live encoder inside the
# process that also serves HTTP and runs the sweeper, and a typo would
# otherwise be found by the OOM killer.
#
# SIZING. Measured on this stack: the app container sits at ~20 MiB idle and
# peaks at ~520 MiB while one slot renders the heaviest clip in the test matrix
# (1080p60 -> 720p). Much of that is page cache for the scratch file and is
# reclaimable, so read it as the ceiling one slot can ask for, not as its
# resident cost. Budget ~0.5 GiB per slot on top of the idle process, and one
# core per slot; the rule of thumb is
#     TRANSCODE_CONCURRENCY <= min(cores, RAM_GiB)
# with APP_MEM_LIMIT below as the backstop, which is what turns "too many
# slots" into a killed container instead of a killed host. DEPLOY.md §7 has
# the measurement method.
#TRANSCODE_CONCURRENCY=1

# libx264 preset and CRF, the frame-rate cap and the frame box.
#
# SINCE THE QUALITY TIERS (DEPLOY.md §27) these bound the card's LIGHT copy and
# nothing else. They no longer cap the file a viewer watches: a playable upload
# is streamed as it was uploaded, and a source the browser cannot play is
# re-encoded at ITS OWN resolution and frame rate (the FULL tier, FULL_CODEC
# below, CRF 18 at TRANSCODE_PRESET). So a 1440p60 capture stays 1440p60.
#
#   PRESET      how much CPU an H.264 Full and the Light copy each spend.
#   CRF         the Light copy's floor only — it is encoded at CRF 26.
#   MAX_FPS     a ceiling the Light copy takes the MINIMUM of with its own 30,
#               and the sanity bound that keeps a container claiming 1000 fps
#               from being believed. It cannot lower a Full below the rate the
#               source itself ran at.
#   MAX_WIDTH   a ceiling the Light copy takes the minimum of with its own
#   MAX_HEIGHT  1280x720 (long side ≤ WIDTH, short side ≤ HEIGHT; both even).
#               Lower them for smaller cards; they never bind a Full.
#TRANSCODE_PRESET=veryfast
#TRANSCODE_CRF=23
#TRANSCODE_MAX_FPS=60
#TRANSCODE_MAX_WIDTH=1280
#TRANSCODE_MAX_HEIGHT=720

# Kill an encode that has reported no progress for this long; the absolute cap
# on one ffmpeg run; and the two attempt budgets (crash-class, environment-class).
#TRANSCODE_STALL_TIMEOUT=5m
#TRANSCODE_HARD_TIMEOUT=6h
#TRANSCODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
#TRANSCODE_MAX_TRANSIENT=10

# How long a job may sit `running` with NO WORKER before the app repairs it
# itself — the same repair `clippet admin jobs unstick` performs by hand
# (DEPLOY.md 24.6). Unset means TRANSCODE_HARD_TIMEOUT + 15m, so 6h15m with the
# default above; 0 switches it off; the floor is 30s. A job this process is
# actually running is never repaired whatever its age, so a SHORTER value is
# fine on a single instance and is what most operators want (5m below); it warns
# once at boot about the only thing it risks, a second process sharing this
# database.
#JOBS_UNSTICK_AFTER=5m

# A ceiling on the FULL tier's bitrate, in BITS per second (2500000 =
# 2.5 Mbps), applied as -maxrate with a -bufsize of twice it. 0 = off: CRF
# alone decides, which is the default and what a player streaming from your
# own bucket wants. Set it if your uplink, not your disk, is the limit — but
# note that it is the one knob that CAN make a Full less than faithful.
#TRANSCODE_MAX_BITRATE=0

# The card's own file: the LIGHT tier, served at /light.mp4 (and still at
# /social.mp4, its original name). The file a crawler would otherwise fetch
# gets a smaller 30 fps copy made for it when that file is at or above this
# many bytes, OR runs above 30 fps, OR is larger than 1280x720 — whatever its
# size. 0 turns the tier off entirely and the card points at the playable file
# itself, exactly as it did before the feature existed. DEPLOY.md §21, §27.
#SOCIAL_MAX_BYTES=26214400

# h264 | av1 — the encoder for the FULL tier: the one stored copy of a source
# no browser can play as it stands (HEVC, ProRes, 10-bit, Matroska, WebM, an
# interlaced capture...). It is encoded at the SOURCE's own resolution and
# frame rate, never downscaled, so this chooses only how those bytes are spent:
#
#   h264  (the default) plays in every browser, every Discord client and every
#         embedded player, and encodes fast.
#   av1   roughly halves the file for the same picture, encodes considerably
#         slower, and wants decoder hardware from about 2019 (Chrome 70+,
#         Firefox 67+, Edge, Safari 17+).
#
# It bounds nothing else: a playable original is never re-encoded, and the
# card's LIGHT copy is h264 whatever this says. `av1` on an ffmpeg without
# libsvtav1 is a BOOT ERROR rather than a clip that fails three times.
# DEPLOY.md §27.
#FULL_CODEC=h264

# How many threads ONE encode may use. 0 — the default — is AUTO, and auto reads
# this container's own CPU quota.
#
# It exists because `nproc` inside a container reports every core the HOST has,
# whatever `cpus:` says — a CFS quota is a share of time, not a cpuset. So on the
# shipped `cpus: 1.0` x264 sized its frame-thread pool for sixteen cores and was
# then throttled to one: measured 25 % SLOWER than the same encode pinned, with
# output 2-5 % larger. Auto is what you want; set a number only if you know
# better than the quota (a `cpus:` you raised for something else, or a bare
# process with no cgroup). The boot line prints the number it resolved.
# DEPLOY.md §27, §20 erratum 215.
#TRANSCODE_THREADS=0

# Hardware encoding. Empty — the default — renders the CPU argv this pipeline has
# always rendered, and is what every install without a graphics card runs.
#
#   cuda   NVIDIA: hardware decode, scale_cuda and h264_nvenc. Measured at
#          9-11x libx264 AT ONE CPU on an RTX 5090, and nearly core-count
#          independent, so it is the one lever that changes the order of
#          magnitude on a small box.
#
# It needs the GPU image (`docker build --target gpu`, selected by the
# COMPOSE_FILE line further down): the ordinary image ships a static ffmpeg with NO nvenc encoder at
# all, and `cuda` on it is a BOOT ERROR rather than a queue of failed clips.
# Most of the filter graph cannot run on the card either, so an HDR, interlaced,
# tag-rotated or anamorphic source silently falls back to the CPU argv on the
# same binary — the `hwaccel` field of the per-clip `planned` log line says which
# ran and why. FULL_CODEC=av1 also stays on the CPU (av1_nvenc is not built).
# DEPLOY.md §27, §20 erratum 216.
#TRANSCODE_HWACCEL=

# The ONE line burned into the card's LIGHT copy: small, semi-transparent,
# bottom-right, naming the preview quality and the real one.
#
# It is the single exception to this product's no-watermark rule and it is
# deliberate (decided 2026-09-08: the light copy is labelled so a preview never
# makes the product look worse than it is — clear yet unobtrusive). A card plays a 720p30 copy of a 1440p60
# capture, and a reader who is not told that concludes the product is a 720p
# product. It is on NOTHING else: never the Original, never the Full, never a
# poster, a preview GIF or anything anybody downloads or the site plays.
#
#   {light}  the card copy's own quality ("720p30")
#   {best}   the quality the link leads to ("1440p60")
#
# Write your own line, or `off` for none — an EMPTY value is the shipped
# default, not off. DEPLOY.md §27, §20 erratum 214.
#LIGHT_TAG_TEXT={light} preview · {best} on Clippet

# The binaries, resolved on PATH at boot (both ship inside the image).
#FFMPEG_PATH=ffmpeg
#FFPROBE_PATH=ffprobe

# --- accounts, sessions, abuse ----------------------------------------------

# open | first | closed.
#
#   first   (the default) accept registration only while NO account exists.
#           The first person to reach the page becomes the account holder and
#           the door closes behind them -- which is what you want the moment
#           the documented tunnel puts this origin on the public internet.
#   open    anybody who finds the hostname can create an account and upload.
#           Set it deliberately, for the evening your friends sign up, and put
#           it back afterwards. Configuration is read at boot, so each flip is
#           a `docker compose up -d`.
#   closed  nobody may register. Add accounts with
#           `docker compose exec -T app clippet admin users create <name>`
#           (the password is read from stdin). See DEPLOY.md section 19.
#
# scripts/e2e.sh REGISTERS THREE THROWAWAY USERS PER RUN, so a stack you run
# the E2E against needs `REGISTRATION=open` here. Step 1 says so and stops.
#REGISTRATION=first

# Who the library lists (DEPLOY.md §20). `shared` — the default, and what every
# version before this one did — means every account sees every other account's
# LISTED clips (unlisted ones were always owner-only). `own` means an account's
# library and GET /api/clips are its own clips only: the answer to "I run this
# for four friends and my job-hunting clip should not be in their library".
# It narrows a LIST and nothing else: a share link still works for anyone who
# has it, and the Discord auto-post still posts every user's clips to the one
# channel it is configured for.
#LIBRARY_SCOPE=shared

# The word this instance wears so you can never mistake it for the production
# one (DEPLOY.md §7.1). Set it on a dev or staging box beside a real one:
#   - the wordmark's green span goes GREY, everywhere the mark appears, the
#     embed overlay included (green span = production, grey span = dev),
#   - the word sits beside the mark in the header and under it on the front
#     door and the login page,
#   - every <title> gains it as a prefix ("dev · Settings · Clippet"),
#   - Discord's site line and the oEmbed provider read "clippet dev", so a
#     card posted from the dev box is not a card posted from production,
#   - an installed app is named "Clippet dev" on the phone.
# Lowercase letters, digits and `-`, at most 12 characters; anything else is a
# boot error. UNSET IS PRODUCTION and changes nothing at all.
#INSTANCE_BADGE=dev

# A clip's page declares its own Discord card, a "component embed" (DEPLOY.md
# §28): the mark, the host, the title, the length, the playable video, the
# state's colour and an Open button. The ordinary card stays on the page and is
# what Discord shows when it cannot draw this one. On by default; `off` leaves
# the ordinary card alone (Discord's format is new and may change, and a Clippet
# Pass name's guard must know the element before it is served through it).
#CARD_COMPONENTS=off

# The /setup wizard (DEPLOY.md knob table); off: 404 but its steps view - the desktop app sets it.
#SETUP_WIZARD=off

# Session lifetime (sliding) and the cookie's name. A __Host- / __Secure-
# prefix requires an https PUBLIC_BASE_URL or the app refuses to boot.
#SESSION_TTL=720h
#SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=clippet_session

# The CEILING on that sliding window, measured from the session's creation. A
# session may slide, but never past `created_at + SESSION_MAX_TTL`, so a stolen
# cookie that is kept warm still dies on a schedule its holder cannot extend.
# Ninety days by default; lower it if you hand the link to a shared machine.
#SESSION_MAX_TTL=2160h

# Comma-separated CIDRs whose X-Real-IP / X-Forwarded-For are trusted
# (cloudflared / reverse proxy). Empty = trust nothing — behind a tunnel that
# collapses every viewer onto the Docker gateway for rate limits and view
# dedupe. Use 172.16.0.0/12 (or the exact bridge subnet) when the app port is
# NOT directly reachable from the internet. See DEPLOY.md §4.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# How long one viewer's repeat visit stops counting, and the cap on how many
# such entries are remembered.
#VIEW_DEDUPE_TTL=30m
#VIEW_DEDUPE_MAX=50000

# Register/login attempts per IP per minute, then the TWO budgets an upload must
# satisfy — per user per hour and per client IP per hour — and the hard cap on
# keys any one limiter will hold. The IP budget is deliberately the looser of
# the pair: it is there because accounts are free (one address can register ~600
# an hour), not to punish a household behind one NAT.
#RATE_AUTH_PER_MIN=10
#RATE_UPLOAD_PER_HOUR=60
#RATE_UPLOAD_PER_HOUR_PER_IP=120
#RATE_LIMIT_MAX_KEYS=10000

# --- integrations ------------------------------------------------------------

# Discord auto-post (DEPLOY.md §11). UNSET = off. Set it and every clip that becomes
# ready — from every user, unlisted ones excepted — is posted to that one
# channel. Server Settings → Integrations → Webhooks → Copy Webhook URL.
# The URL is a CREDENTIAL (its token is the last path segment): treat .env as a
# secret, and delete + recreate the webhook in Discord if it ever leaks.
#DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/<id>/<token>

# Per-tag channel routing (DEPLOY.md §25). `<tag>=<url>` rules separated by
# semicolons: a clip is posted to the FIRST rule whose tag it carries, and the
# order here IS the priority. Every URL is validated exactly as
# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL is (https, a real Discord host, /api/webhooks/<id>/<token>)
# and every one of them is a CREDENTIAL. Tags follow the tag grammar
# (a-z, 0-9 and -, up to 32 characters); a tag that could never exist fails the
# boot rather than silently never firing, and `default` is RESERVED (it already
# names the DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL channel in the logs and on a queued job). A rule
# the app refuses is named by its POSITION here, never by quoting what you
# typed, so a rule written backwards still prints no token.
#DISCORD_WEBHOOK_ROUTES=clutch=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/<id>/<token>;fails=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/<id2>/<token2>

# What happens to a clip no route matched: `default` posts it to
# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL (and posts nothing if that is unset), `skip` posts it
# nowhere at all — which is how you say "only tagged clips are announced".
# `skip` needs rules: with DISCORD_WEBHOOK_ROUTES empty and a live
# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL it would announce nothing at all, so it fails the boot.
#DISCORD_WEBHOOK_UNMATCHED=default

# Clip webhook (DEPLOY.md §11.1). UNSET = off. Set both and every clip that
# becomes ready — from every user, unlisted ones included — is POSTed to this
# URL as a small JSON document, signed with the secret.
# http or https. The URL is a CREDENTIAL. The secret is 32 characters or more.
#CLIP_WEBHOOK_URL=https://automation.example.com/hooks/clippet
#CLIP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The Discord BOT (DEPLOY.md §30) — a different feature from the Discord webhook above:
# it answers `/upload`, `/clip` and the Apps-menu `Enrich` IN a server. All three or none. With your OWN application
# (§30.1–§30.7) the token is the one SECRET of the three. To use the OFFICIAL
# Clippet bot instead (§30.8): the id and key printed on its claim page, and
# the literal word `managed` as the token — this instance then publishes no
# command and sends no token anywhere; run `clippet admin bot bind` and
# connect the server on the claim page.
#DISCORD_APP_ID=123456789012345678
#DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=<64 hex characters>
#DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=managed

# The two commands' NAMES, without the slash: DISCORD_COMMAND_NAME is
# `/clip link:` (default `clip`), DISCORD_UPLOAD_COMMAND_NAME is `/upload file:`
# (default `upload`). Each is 1–32 characters of lowercase letters, digits, `-`
# and `_` (Discord's own rule), and the two must differ. One command per name
# per server, so give a second instance second names — a dev box on `clip-dev`
# and `upload-dev` beside a production box on the defaults. Setting either with
# the bot off is a boot error, and so is any name but the default on the
# OFFICIAL bot, which publishes `/upload` and `/clip` globally (DEPLOY.md §30.2,
# §30.8). Re-run `clippet admin bot register` after changing them: the names
# live in the commands Discord STORES, and that call replaces the server's whole
# command set, so the old names go with it.
#DISCORD_COMMAND_NAME=clip-dev
#DISCORD_UPLOAD_COMMAND_NAME=upload-dev
# The third, the MESSAGE command — right-click (long-press) a message → Apps →
# Enrich posts the card for the first link to a clip here in that message.
# Default `Enrich`; a message command's name may carry uppercase and spaces,
# 1–32 characters. Same orphan and official-bot rules as the two above.
#DISCORD_ENRICH_COMMAND_NAME=Enrich (dev)
# The fourth, `/settings` — visible only to members with Manage Server; its
# panel opens this server's account's link defaults (how long a clip is kept,
# listed or not, the link's shape) in a form (DEPLOY.md §30.9). Default
# `settings`; the slash-command rule, and it must differ from the two names
# above. Same orphan and official-bot rules.
#DISCORD_SETTINGS_COMMAND_NAME=settings-dev
# /upload opens a form - Title, Delete after, Tags - while the upload runs
# (DEPLOY.md §30.5). off: no form; every upload gets the account's defaults.
#DISCORD_UPLOAD_FORM=off
# Enrich on a message with a video and no link to a clip here uploads that
# video, for its poster or Manage Server (DEPLOY.md §30.4). Off by default.
#DISCORD_ENRICH_UPLOAD=on

# Clippet Auto on this box (DEPLOY.md §30.10) — a separate, optional app that
# reads the channels of the servers bound with `admin bot bind`: prompt = one
# reply with an `Upload to Clippet` button under each video, auto = every
# video becomes a link with no press; the original is removed (Manage
# Messages). Needs the bot above (your own app, or DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=managed);
# off opens no socket. The token is the Auto app's bot token, a SECRET.
#DISCORD_AUTO_MODE=prompt
#DISCORD_AUTO_TOKEN=
#DISCORD_AUTO_SECONDS=10
#DISCORD_AUTO_NOTICE=off

# Sign in with Discord (DEPLOY.md §19.6) — a different feature from both of
# the above: a "Sign in with Discord" button on /login that opens an account
# somebody has LINKED (from Settings with their password, or with
# `clippet admin users link <username> <discord-id>`). It never creates an
# account, under any REGISTRATION. Both or none; one alone is a boot error.
# The client id is the application id (an own-bot instance may reuse its
# DISCORD_APP_ID); the secret is Developer Portal → OAuth2 → Reset Secret and
# is the one SECRET of the two. Add `<PUBLIC_BASE_URL>/api/auth/discord/callback`
# under OAuth2 → Redirects, byte for byte; `clippet admin doctor` prints it.
#DISCORD_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID=123456789012345678
#DISCORD_LOGIN_CLIENT_SECRET=<the client secret>

# --- process -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Deadline for the share/embed page's database read.
#SSR_TIMEOUT=5s

# debug|info|warn|error, and text|json (json is what you ship to a log system).
#LOG_LEVEL=info
#LOG_FORMAT=text

# The release to run -- a COMPOSE knob, not an app variable. compose.yaml pulls
# ghcr.io/clippet-gg/clippet:<this value>, and the newest release when it is
# unset; set it to pin one, for example CLIPPET_VERSION=2026.11.07. A published
# release is named `YYYY.MM.DD`, with `.N` for a second one on a day (DEPLOY.md
# §17.4).
#
# When you build from source (the compose.build.yaml overlay), it is also a
# build input: the version string baked into the binary (`clippet version`,
# /healthz's `version`). With it unset every local build reports "dev", so an
# upgrade cannot be confirmed from the outside (DEPLOY.md §16). `git describe
# --always --dirty` is the obvious value there. It is read at BUILD time, so
# changing it needs `docker compose up -d --build`. Set it beside CLIPPET_COMMIT
# below.
#CLIPPET_VERSION=2026.11.07

# The COMMIT that version was built from — `git rev-parse HEAD`, a second
# COMPOSE knob, a build input of the compose.build.yaml overlay beside
# CLIPPET_VERSION; a published image already carries its own (DEPLOY.md §17.4, DESIGN.md
# §20 erratum 303). `GET /api/instance` publishes it, which is how a Clippet
# Pass name knows which release is answering (DEPLOY.md §36); empty — the
# default — is published as `null`, and so is anything that is not forty
# lower-case hex characters. It is read at BUILD time, like the line above, and
# `scripts/e2e.sh` exports it for every stack it builds so a gate's own stack
# always carries one.
#
# PASTE THE FORTY CHARACTERS, do not write `$(git rev-parse HEAD)` here: compose
# does not run a shell over this file, so a substitution is stored and passed to
# the build literally and is published as `null` — the same answer as leaving
# the line out. To let the shell do it, put it in front of the build instead:
# `CLIPPET_COMMIT=$(git rev-parse HEAD) docker compose up -d --build`. The same
# is true of CLIPPET_VERSION above.
#CLIPPET_COMMIT=

# The image compose runs -- a COMPOSE knob, not an app variable. It wins over
# CLIPPET_VERSION in compose.yaml and over the overlay's own tag in
# compose.build.yaml (clippet-app:local when unset). Overridable so a
# side-by-side checkout (another worktree, another clone) builds and runs its
# own tag instead of colliding with this one's.
#CLIPPET_IMAGE=ghcr.io/clippet-gg/clippet:2026.11.07

# The GPU flavour (DEPLOY.md §27, DESIGN.md §20 erratum 247): `compose.gpu.yaml`
# OVERRIDES the one `app` service -- a build of the Dockerfile's `gpu` target,
# its own tag, and the card granted -- so selecting it here is the whole switch:
# every `docker compose up -d --build` from then on is the GPU build, and every
# command that says `app` still means the app. COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR keeps the
# two-file list portable (Windows would otherwise read it with ";"). Unused,
# unresolved and unpulled unless you uncomment the lines.
#
# To pull the GPU image instead of building it, set CLIPPET_GPU_IMAGE to a
# published tag and run `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`.
# ghcr.io/clippet-gg/clippet:gpu-<version> tags exist from 2026.11.07, and
# ghcr.io/clippet-gg/clippet:gpu-latest from the next release.
#COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR=:
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.gpu.yaml
#CLIPPET_GPU_IMAGE=clippet-app:gpu

# A Cloudflare NAMED tunnel as a compose service (DEPLOY.md §3): add
# `compose.tunnel.yaml` to COMPOSE_FILE and paste the dashboard's connector token. Selecting the
# file without the token is a loud compose error. PUBLIC_BASE_URL then names the real domain.
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.tunnel.yaml
#CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=eyJ...

# A CLIPPET PASS name as a compose service (DEPLOY.md §36, DESIGN.md §20 erratum 303) — optional
# and paid, and this instance is complete without it. Add `compose.pass.yaml` to COMPOSE_FILE and
# paste the token your Clippet portal gave you; selecting the file without the token is a loud
# compose error. The token is the sidecar's, not this app's: no `internal/config` reader ever sees
# it, exactly as with the tunnel's token above.
#
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL and SESSION_COOKIE_NAME are the APP's own half and must be set from the FIRST
# boot on the name. The media has to be in a bucket with MEDIA_DELIVERY=redirect, which the /setup
# wizard's third `Reached` answer prints as one whole block.
#COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR=:
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.nobucket.yaml:compose.pass.yaml
#CLIPPET_PASS_TOKEN=<the token from your Clippet portal>

# Resource ceilings for the app container -- COMPOSE knobs (the v2
# `mem_limit`/`cpus` keys; a `deploy.resources` block is Swarm-only and is
# ignored by `docker compose up`). One process runs the HTTP server, the
# sweeper and every ffmpeg, so without a limit an encode that overshoots takes
# the whole instance rather than one job; with one, the container is killed and
# `restart: unless-stopped` brings it back, and the interrupted transcode is
# released to the queue without counting an attempt (DEPLOY.md §7, §16).
# Size them from TRANSCODE_CONCURRENCY: about 0.5 GiB and one core per slot,
# plus a little for the process itself. The 2g default carries the default
# single slot with room to spare.
# APP_CPUS MUST NOT EXCEED THE HOST'S CORE COUNT: the daemon tolerates a
# mem_limit above physical RAM, but a `cpus` above the core count is refused at
# container CREATION ("range of CPUs is from 0.01 to N, as there are only N
# CPUs available") and `docker compose up -d` leaves you with no app container
# at all. `nproc` is the ceiling; the 1.0 default is safe on a 1-vCPU box.
#APP_MEM_LIMIT=2g
#APP_CPUS=1.0

# Container-internal wiring: coupled to compose's port mapping, named volumes
# and healthcheck. Settable, but changing one needs a matching edit there.
#CLIPPET_LISTEN=:8080
#DATA_DIR=/data
#SCRATCH_DIR=/scratch

# Host side of the app's published port — a COMPOSE knob, not an app variable
# (the container always listens on CLIPPET_LISTEN, `:8080`). The default binds
# LOOPBACK ONLY, which is what makes TRUSTED_PROXIES above safe: nothing off
# this machine can reach the port, so nothing but your proxy can claim to be a
# client. `curl http://localhost:8481/readyz`, scripts/e2e.sh and a cloudflared
# reaching host.docker.internal all still work. Widen it only when something
# off-box must reach the app DIRECTLY (a LAN box with no proxy, a cloudflared
# on another host) — and then leave TRUSTED_PROXIES empty, because a reachable
# port plus a trusted prefix lets any caller spoof X-Real-IP past every per-IP
# limit and every view-dedupe key (DEPLOY.md §1, §4). `clippet admin doctor`
# reads this variable when it is in the environment you hand it and FAILS on
# the dangerous combination, which is one reason to run it with
# `--env-file .env` from the host rather than inside the container, where a
# compose-only knob is not visible (DEPLOY.md §24.1):
#   CLIPPET_PUBLISH=0.0.0.0:8481
#CLIPPET_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:8481
